            "An obstructed airway kills in three to five minutes, which is why it sits at the top of every priority ladder and why the first question in every emergency is simply <strong>'can the patient talk?'</strong> A clear sentence proves the airway is open, the patient is breathing and the brain is perfused; anything less demands a closer look. The commonest cause of obstruction in the emergency unit is not a foreign body but the <strong>tongue of an unconscious patient</strong> falling back against the posterior pharynx when the muscles relax — after a head injury, a stroke, an overdose, a seizure, hypoglycaemia, anaesthesia or alcohol — and the second commonest is <strong>fluid</strong>: vomit, blood and secretions that an unconscious patient cannot cough or swallow. Then come <strong>swelling</strong> (anaphylaxis, inhalation burns, epiglottitis and deep neck infections such as Ludwig's angina from a dental abscess, angio-oedema from an ACE inhibitor, a neck haematoma after thyroid surgery), <strong>foreign bodies</strong> (groundnuts, pieces of meat, coins, dentures, a child's toy), <strong>trauma</strong> to the face and larynx, and <strong>laryngospasm</strong>. The patient with a <strong>Glasgow Coma Scale of 8 or less</strong> cannot protect their airway from their own tongue and their own vomit even when it sounds clear now, and needs an adjunct, a position and a plan for a definitive airway — 'less than eight, intubate' is a reminder, not a rule, but the risk is real.",
