            "The <strong>head tilt–chin lift</strong> is the first manoeuvre for every patient without suspected spinal injury: one hand on the forehead tilts the head back, two fingers under the bony chin lift it forward, pulling the tongue off the posterior pharynx; in infants the head is kept in a <strong>neutral 'sniffing' position</strong> because extension kinks their soft trachea. When the mechanism suggests cervical spine injury — a road crash, a fall from a height, diving, an unconscious trauma patient, neck pain or neurological signs — use the <strong>jaw thrust</strong>: fingers behind the angles of the mandible on both sides push the jaw forward and up while the head stays in neutral alignment held by an assistant, a collar or blocks. If the jaw thrust fails, a careful head tilt is added, because a dead patient with an intact spine helps nobody. Clear visible material with a gloved finger sweep only if you can see it; blind finger sweeps push foreign bodies deeper.",
            "<strong>Suction</strong> removes what manoeuvres cannot. A rigid <strong>Yankauer</strong> sucker clears the mouth and pharynx under direct vision; a soft flexible catheter is used through the nose, an airway adjunct or a tracheostomy, inserted without suction applied and withdrawn with a rotating movement, each pass limited to <strong>10–15 seconds</strong> (count out loud) with oxygen before and between passes, at a pressure of about 80–120 mmHg in adults and lower in children, because suction removes oxygen as well as secretions and stimulates the vagus — hypoxia, bradycardia, vomiting and laryngospasm are its complications. Never suction blindly in suspected epiglottitis; never insert a catheter through the nose after a base-of-skull fracture. If a child is choking on a visible object and suction is unavailable, position them head down and use back blows rather than fingers.",
