            "Oxygen is prescribed, like any drug, with a device, a flow and a <strong>target saturation</strong>, but in the first minutes of a critical illness every patient receives <strong>high-flow oxygen through a non-rebreather mask at 15 litres per minute</strong> and the device is then titrated down to the target once the patient is stable. The targets are <strong>94–98%</strong> for most patients and <strong>88–92%</strong> for patients at risk of carbon dioxide retention — chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, severe kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular disease, obesity hypoventilation — in whom uncontrolled oxygen can slowly abolish the hypoxic drive and worsen ventilation–perfusion matching, producing a patient who becomes drowsy and acidotic over hours. The lesson from that risk is <strong>titration and monitoring</strong>, not withholding: a hypoxic patient with COPD still receives oxygen, from a Venturi mask or nasal cannula at a controlled flow, with the saturation checked and a blood gas if they become drowsy; nobody has died of a saturation of 92%, and many have died of hypoxia while a nurse worried about the hypoxic drive.",
