            "A patient who is apnoeic, gasping, or breathing fewer than 8–10 times a minute with a falling saturation needs ventilating, and the <strong>bag-valve-mask</strong> (Ambu bag) with oxygen at 15 L/min and the reservoir attached is the tool. Technique decides success: insert an oropharyngeal airway, position the head (tilt or jaw thrust as appropriate), choose a mask that seals from the bridge of the nose to the cleft of the chin, and hold it with the <strong>E–C grip</strong> — thumb and index finger in a C pressing the mask down, the other three fingers in an E lifting the jaw up into the mask. A seal is hard to achieve alone, so the <strong>two-person technique</strong> — one holding the mask with both hands and thrusting the jaw, the other squeezing — is used whenever a second person exists. Squeeze <strong>just enough to see the chest rise</strong>, over about one second, roughly 500–600 mL in an adult, at <strong>10–12 breaths a minute</strong> (one every five to six seconds) in an adult who is not in cardiac arrest and 12–20 in a child; in cardiac arrest with an advanced airway the rate is 10 a minute with continuous compressions. <strong>Over-ventilation</strong> — too fast, too hard — is the common error and it harms: it inflates the stomach and causes vomiting and aspiration, raises the pressure in the chest and reduces venous return and cardiac output, and lowers the carbon dioxide so that cerebral vessels constrict. If the chest does not rise, reposition the head, improve the seal, check for obstruction, consider a two-person grip, and in trauma re-check the jaw thrust before giving up on the airway.",
