          paragraphs: [
            "In an emergency the aim is <strong>two large-bore cannulae in large veins</strong> as fast as possible, because flow through a tube depends on its radius to the fourth power and on its length: a short, wide peripheral cannula in the antecubital fossa delivers fluid faster than a long central line. The gauges and their approximate maximum flows are <strong>14 G orange about 250–300 mL/min, 16 G grey about 180–200, 18 G green about 90–100, 20 G pink about 60, 22 G blue about 35, 24 G yellow about 20</strong>; a shocked adult needs 14 or 16 G, a child the largest the vein will take, and a 22 G in the back of the hand is for antibiotics, not for resuscitation. Choose the antecubital fossa, the forearm or the external jugular in the collapsed patient; avoid a limb with a fracture, a burn, an arteriovenous fistula, lymphoedema or on the side of a mastectomy, and avoid the lower limbs in adults if anything else is available. Draw blood at the moment of cannulation — full blood count, group and cross-match (two separately labelled samples where the policy demands), urea and electrolytes, glucose, clotting, lactate, a pregnancy test in any woman who could be pregnant — because the second attempt is harder once the veins have collapsed further. Flush, secure with a transparent dressing so the site can be seen, label with the date and time, and document the gauge and site.",
            "When peripheral access fails in a patient who needs it now — <strong>two attempts or 90 seconds in a shocked child</strong>, a similar limit in a collapsed adult — the <strong>intraosseous route</strong> is the answer, not a third and fourth attempt. The marrow cavity is a non-collapsible vein: any drug, fluid or blood that can be given intravenously can be given intraosseously at the same dose and reaches the central circulation in seconds. The usual site is the <strong>proximal tibia</strong>, 1–2 cm below and medial to the tibial tuberosity on the flat anteromedial surface (away from the growth plate in children); alternatives are the distal femur, the distal tibia above the medial malleolus and the humeral head in adults. A manual needle or a battery-powered drill is inserted at 90 degrees until the give of the cortex is felt; placement is confirmed by the needle standing firm, aspiration of marrow (not always possible) and a flush that runs without swelling; the first flush of 5–10 mL of saline (2–5 mL in children) is painful in the conscious patient and 2% lidocaine through the needle before it helps. Fluids will not run by gravity — use a <strong>pressure bag or push them with a syringe</strong> — and the limb is watched continuously for swelling, because <strong>extravasation into the calf causes compartment syndrome</strong>. The needle is removed within 24 hours once venous access is obtained; its complications are extravasation, fracture, infection and, rarely, osteomyelitis. A limb with a fracture, a previous intraosseous attempt in the last 48 hours, infection over the site or bone disease is not used.",
            "<strong>Central venous access</strong> — the internal jugular, subclavian or femoral vein — is a doctor's procedure, but its safety depends on the nurse: a full aseptic bundle (hand hygiene, chlorhexidine skin preparation, sterile gown, gloves, mask and a large drape, a checklist), the patient positioned <strong>head-down (Trendelenburg) for neck and subclavian lines</strong> so the veins fill and air cannot be sucked in, continuous ECG monitoring during insertion because the guidewire triggers arrhythmias, and a <strong>chest X-ray to confirm the tip and exclude a pneumothorax before the line is used</strong> except in a resuscitation. Every lumen is flushed and labelled, blood return is checked before drugs are given, connections are Luer-locked and clamped when open, the dressing is transparent and changed every seven days or when soiled, and the line is removed as soon as it is not needed. <strong>Air embolism</strong> — sudden breathlessness, chest pain, cyanosis, a 'mill-wheel' murmur and collapse during insertion, a dressing change or an accidental disconnection — is treated by clamping or covering the line, turning the patient onto the <strong>left side, head down</strong> to trap air in the right ventricle, giving 100% oxygen and calling for help; it is prevented by clamping before disconnecting, closed systems, head-down positioning when the line is open to air, and removing central lines with the patient supine or head-down on expiration with an occlusive dressing applied for 24 hours.",
          ],
